## Embedded model weights

`module/src/*.bin` are sentence-embedding weights, quantised and repacked into a fixed
binary layout that the no_std module reads sequentially. The layout, the packing tools
and the inference code are ours. The weights are not.

| Blob | Model | Licence | Text |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `minilm.bin` (22.9 MB) | `sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2` | Apache-2.0 | `LICENSES/Apache-2.0-all-MiniLM-L6-v2.txt` |
| `gte-small.bin` (27.9 MB) | `thenlper/gte-small` | MIT | `LICENSES/MIT-thenlper-gte-small.txt` |
| `vectors.bin`, `vectors-glove.bin` | GloVe 6B, Pennington, Socher and Manning 2014, Stanford NLP | PDDL-1.0 | `LICENSES/PDDL-1.0-GloVe.txt` |

Apache-2.0 requires its notice to travel with the work, which is what this entry and
that licence file do. `module/src/minilm.rs` reimplements the MiniLM forward pass from
the published architecture; the code is ours, the trained parameters are Apache-2.0.

### `vectors-champ.bin` is a distillation of another module and is not published

`vectors-champ.bin` (11.6 MB) is a table of 384-dimensional vectors obtained by running
words through the `embed()` export of the Telegraph baseline scoring module, which is
`telegraphprotocol/telegraph-wasm-baseline`, MIT © 2026 telegraphprotocol
(`LICENSES/MIT-telegraphprotocol-wasm-baseline.txt`). The vectors are that model's
outputs, not ours.

MIT would permit publishing it. It is still removed from this repository, for two
reasons worth stating plainly: it is derived parameter data from someone else's model
rather than anything we trained, and it is dead weight, since nothing in the tree reads
it (`lib.rs` embeds `vectors.bin`, `minilm.rs` embeds `minilm.bin`). The method that
produced it stays documented in `worklogs/LEDGER.md` under 2026-08-18, and
`reference/distill` regenerates it from the baseline module in an afternoon. Removing
the artefact does not hide anything: the recipe is published, the source model is
public and MIT.

